{"conversation":"Ah, I did not consider duplicate subsequences formed by repeated characters. If a character appears again, just doubling and adding 1 will count some subsequences twice. I think I need to remember the last position each character was seen to subtract the duplicated part formed last time this character was added. But I'm not sure how to implement that.","python_code":"class Solution:
    def distinctSubseqII(self, s: str) -> int:
        subseq_count = 0
        last_pos = {}
        for i in range(len(s)):
            subseq_count = subseq_count * 2 + 1
            c = s[i]
            if c in last_pos:
                subseq_count -= last_pos[c]
            last_pos[c] = subseq_count
        return subseq_count"}

{"conversation":"I see, so last_pos should probably store the count of subsequences that ended with the character at its last position. Then when we come to the same character again, we subtract those subsequences to avoid double counting. I will try to fix last_pos updates and also add modulo operation as the problem says answer can be very large.","python_code":"class Solution:
    def distinctSubseqII(self, s: str) -> int:
        MOD = 10**9 + 7
        subseq_count = 0
        last_pos = {}
        for c in s:
            new_count = (subseq_count * 2 + 1) % MOD
            if c in last_pos:
                new_count = (new_count - last_pos[c]) % MOD
            last_pos[c] = (subseq_count + 1) % MOD
            subseq_count = new_count
        return subseq_count"}
